--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md
@@ -7235,6 +7235,8 @@ The first therapeutic run confirmed that our initial cure correctly made foundin
 
 The follow-up cure also addresses Workers that were genuinely needed when delivered but later completed every useful task. Base AdvCiv forced every revealed landmass above 3 tiles to retain at least 1 Worker, so a completed secondary island never reached zero demand; owned cities also caused the stranded-unit logic to reject its Worker from naval rescue. The primary landmass still keeps this reserve, but a completed secondary landmass can now reach zero Worker demand. Only after ordinary Worker logic finds no productive local action, the Worker moves to a suitable coast, advertises itself for pickup and waits there. An available empty Settler transport can collect it without interrupting an active founding mission. The first transport assignment reserves that individual Worker so additional ships remain available for other duties or other completed-area Workers.
 
+The completed-secondary-landmass rule does not apply to Barbarians. Their separate islands do not use the normal civilization system that collects a finished Worker and reassigns it to another landmass. In addition, a Barbarian city's best-build cache can remain empty until it has a Worker; without the inherited local reserve, both the area and city calculations can report zero Worker demand, so `AI_barbChooseProduction` never produces the Worker needed to reveal and perform those local jobs. Barbarians therefore keep the inherited local Worker reserve in both calculations, while ordinary civilizations retain the completed-secondary-landmass behavior above. In the confirming `BBAI_20260720T193721Z_load1.log`, Barbarian cities completed 13 Workers across 11 cities, preserving their ability to develop their separate islands.
+
 The confirming `BBAI_20260720T150846Z_load1.log` recorded 14 completed-area Worker boardings. Persia recovered all 5 Workers from its completed islands on turn 226, including 2 that boarded available transports directly and 3 collected through the stranded-unit pickup path. No two distinct transports targeted the same Worker during the same turn after the coordination guard. If no empty transport is available, the Worker waits rather than forcing the AI to build a ship or divert one carrying useful cargo solely for that rescue. Worker demand rises normally again if losses, new technology, expansion or other changes create useful work on the landmass.
 
 Fixed/improved with the help of GPT-5.6-Sol (on ChatGPT Codex) thanks.

diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
index 64459f989..65b1179e1 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
@@ -158,6 +158,7 @@ Some features are not supported in AdvCiv-SAS; e.g., non-English languages, pre-
 - Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, SAS game-summary log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_SUMMARY_TURN_INTERVAL` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. For stronger LLM analysis, also provide compact mod context when possible; for example, the AdvCiv-SAS light source ZIP from [`make_light_source_zip.py`](/LLM_Helpers/README.md#make_light_source_zippy) includes useful source/XML/docs/helper context while staying compact enough to upload and share. See also [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
 - **BBAI worker log split:** added separate worker and Work Boat / `UNITAI_WORKER_SEA` BBAI log-level defines so ordinary `SAS_BBAI_UNIT_LOG_LEVEL` can focus on non-worker unit behavior such as scrapping, army movement, and combat-unit decisions. Worker and worker-sea logs had become noisy enough to hide those unit decisions, and Work Boats use distinct logic from land workers, so they now have their own category as well.
 - **BBAI Settler log split:** added `SAS_BBAI_SETTLER_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT so Settlers and other units can be inspected independently. This notably covers city-site decisions, Settler movement, scouting, returns, founding, (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) parking reasons, danger context, and city escort pools; FOUND as of now separately controls detailed city-site scoring calculations. This notably lets UNIT investigate other unit behavior without enabling the multi-megabyte first-city Settler traces needed for example for the Berlin, Karakorum, and Cuzco regression tests.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6062+) **Overseas-transport BBAI diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_OVERSEAS_TRANSPORT_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from CITY and UNIT so overseas settlement and conquest logistics can be inspected without enabling those broad logs. It notably connects known valuable island sites and cities with their distance and defenders, map type, transport production and cargo capacity, loaded units, units actually available to board, incoming cargo already assigned within 1/3/7 turns, pickup/loading failures, launch thresholds and decisions, naval reachability, and Settler/Worker cargo handling.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6034+) **BBAI War diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_WAR_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from TEAM so UWAI war-target choice can be inspected without broad team/diplomacy logs. It notably records target utility, limited-vs-total war comparison, naval/preparation context, forced peace, attitude, closeness, city distance, power, selected target, target switches, direct-war adoption, war-plan lifecycle changes, and optional high-utility scenario/aspect breakdowns, which should help investigate problematic faraway wars before changing behavior.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6031+) **Great General BBAI diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_GREAT_GENERAL_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT notably so Military Academy construction, Military Instructor joining, Great General unit attachment, target-city choice, and target-unit choice can be inspected together without enabling broad unit logs. This notably helped isolate a bad France sample where `AI_join` rejected all cities for safety/path reasons, then the plot-danger branch attached a Great General to a low-value Longbowman.
 - **Culture-victory BBAI diagnostics:** added a new dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CULTURE_LOG_LEVEL` notably for diagnosing premature, uneven, or wasteful AI culture investment without enabling broad player/city logs. It records for example culture-stage reasons, current/projected Legendary-city countdowns and rival ranking, competing victory progress, actual culture-slider decisions, per-city weights and production, and culture contributed by production processes and assigned specialists.
@@ -360,6 +361,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - **Reliable Worker availability without early overproduction:** AI cities rebuild enough Workers for each land area before other production priorities can leave them short indefinitely, without producing more than the AI calculates it needs. Maintaining enough Workers throughout the game lets the AI improve food/production/commerce, connect resources and routes, repair pillaging, and develop new or underdeveloped cities sooner, especially after Worker losses or earlier underproduction. This normally aims for 1 Worker per city plus a capital-area reserve. As a refinement, a one-city empire needs at most 1 land Worker until its capital has ever reached a tunable population (5 by default), avoiding early double-Worker production and the resulting halted growth; normal demand resumes after the capital matures or another city is founded. Ordinary Worker choices, cases where a blocked Settler choice falls back to a Worker, general unit selection, and Worker scrapping now use the same centralized target. Four BBAI test runs then completed exactly 1 early Worker per empire, with the only later automatic second Worker beginning when its capital reached population 5.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6060+) **Workers are no longer delivered to landmasses that already have enough:** in save file 450, BBAI logging showed a Worker accompanying the Settler to the island where Thapsus was founded, then remaining idle there for 93 turns because none of its plots could be improved. Our first attempt at this fix correctly left an unnecessary Worker on the large landmass (i.e. home) instead of sending it to the small unimprovable island, but exposed a separate problem: general Worker logic kept loading the Worker back onto the island-bound Settler transport, so our early fix had to unload the same Worker back onto the large landmass 14 times. After our second version correctly stopped that loop, testing exposed a third failure: a separate Worker-ferry routine still kept sending extra Workers to islands that already had enough, eventually crowding 5 Workers onto Sardis's two-tile landmass and 2 onto Ergili's three-tile landmass, where most had no useful work. To fix this, Settler transports now leave Workers beyond the destination's exact shortage in their original city, unload only the required number at the destination, and keep any excess aboard for another destination. Workers cannot repeatedly reboard the same transport after being left behind, and later Worker ferries now stop once the destination landmass has enough. These checks use current need and current Worker count, so delivery becomes eligible again if losses leave the landmass short or new work requires workers again. In the confirming run, the AI correctly left a Worker on its origin landmass because the destination needed 1 but already had 3. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6061+) **Workers are brought home after finishing every useful task on a secondary landmass:** Base AdvCiv forced most landmasses to retain at least 1 Worker forever, even after a Worker had legitimately finished developing a small island and could contribute elsewhere. Secondary landmasses can now reach zero Worker demand when no useful task remains. Their finished Workers move to the coast and request pickup; one available Settler transport collects each Worker for reuse elsewhere, rather than several ships pursuing the same rescue. The primary landmass keeps its Worker reserve, and demand on a secondary landmass automatically returns if losses or new work require another Worker. In the confirming save file 450 run, Persia recovered all 5 Workers from its completed islands on the same turn, and 14 completed-area Worker boardings were recorded across the game. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6062+) Note: the above zero-demand and pickup rule above does not apply to the Barbarian player, as Barbarian city improvement planning can report no local jobs until a Worker already exists, and Barbarians do not use the ordinary civilization system to transport and reassign finished Workers between separate landmasses; therefore each isolated Barbarian island keeps local Worker demand (else no Worker is built and Barbarian cities are not improved at all).
 - **Yield-first build execution:** workers build the exact improvement selected through the full city/plot evaluation instead of letting the older `AI_betterPlotBuild` postprocessing replace a scored Chop, Mine, Farm, etc. with an unrelated Road. Roads/routes remain strongly de-prioritized early unless they are truly the best move, while important connectable bonuses are still connected. See [KI#31](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#31---attemptingly-fixed-deprioritize-routes-for-ai-workers-in-favour-of-yields-first-except-in-some-rare-cases).
 - **Overhaul: `AI_bestCityBuild` (full routine):** "holy" improvements (never overwrite: **hamlet, village, town, workshop**) and "semi-holy" (contextual: **farms** in food-poor cities, **cottages** in food-rich cities); fast/ordered improvement; **bonus-specific rules** (e.g., don’t cottage bananas; prefer plantation or a farm only if it makes clear sense); **terrain-aware cottage/farm logic** (flood plains favored for cottages unless the city is food-poor); **minimal oscillation**; prefer steady growth; avoid overwriting until other workable tiles are improved; hold top tiles for later if the "now" move would be inferior; prefer farms on low-food BFCs. See [KI#33](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#33---tremendously-improved-and-fixed-and-enhanced-ai-worker-buildimprovement-logic-in-cvunitaiai_bestcitybuild). Update: worker AI now also weighs the **longer-term value** of strong **non-bonus farms** for **low-food or hill-heavy cities**: they can **support food-consuming mines**, **faster growth**, **more aggressive early slaving**, or **specialist economies**; concrete dry-farm irrigation-chain fixes can replace the least harmful blocker first; and the logic is more **mindful of preserving useful irrigation paths** instead of blindly maximizing immediate cottage/workshop yield (**Reserve** likely irrigation/farm connector plots **from non-farm builds** to reduce AI worker farm/non-farm oscillation).
 - **Worker lower-food replacement guard:** from a **tunable era onward** (default as of now: Medieval), AI workers no longer replace existing **no-bonus** improvements with lower-food alternatives. This targets **repeated food-threshold oscillation** such as **Farm → Cottage → Farm → Cottage** or **Mine → Windmill → Mine** after the city briefly looks food-safe. It still allows equal-food retools, food-restoration changes such as Cottage → Farm, and cleanup of City Ruins or obsolete/non-active improvements. In the tested BBAI log, the worst Mali farm/cottage loops reached Farm again and then skipped later Farm → Cottage attempts from about T128/T130 onward. This change is because before this era, cities are often small or health/happiness-capped (extra/excess food may not necessarily help or could be inefficient early) and early cottages still have time to mature, but later, cities are usually larger, health sources are generally multiple, and specialist yields are stronger than plot yields (so any food can be used and is generally strong/stronger), and cottages/growth improvements have less time to grow and pay back, so preserving food support is usually stronger.

